module sh.sysl.ui_sdl

import sh.sysl.ui.Rect

// The arithmetic between a window and an interface, which is the whole of what a phone changes.
//
// **A tree is built and measured in points, and a window is pixels.** How many pixels a point is
// depends on the display, and where a pointer is reported depends on the platform — so the three
// small conversions below are the entire difference between an interface drawn on a laptop and the
// same one drawn on a phone. They are ordinary functions rather than lines inside the loop because
// they are the part that can be wrong without crashing, and a wrong one is a whole interface at the
// wrong size.

// The scale to draw at, from what the display says.
//
// **Never less than one.** SDL answers `0.0` when it cannot tell and the binding already substitutes
// one; a zero reaching a layout would multiply every size in it to nothing, which is a whole
// interface drawn correctly at no size at all.
scale_of(display: f32) -> real
    val k = real(display)

    if k < 1.0 then 1.0 else k
end scale_of

// How far a pointer coordinate has to be multiplied to land in points.
//
// **SDL reports the pointer in window units and draws in pixels, and on a high-density display those
// are not the same.** The ratio is measured from the two sizes rather than assumed, because which
// convention a platform uses is exactly the thing nothing documents the same way twice: on one the
// window is already pixels and the ratio is one, on another it is the density.
point_scale(window_w: int, pixel_w: int, k: real) -> real
    if window_w <= 0 || pixel_w <= 0 then return 1.0 / k

    real(pixel_w) / real(window_w) / k
end point_scale

// The rectangle an interface is drawn in: the window in points, less the system bars.
//
// **The bars are in pixels and the interface is in points**, so they are divided on the way in. On a
// desktop they are all zero and this is the whole window.
frame_rect(pixel_w: int, pixel_h: int, k: real, left: int, top: int, right: int,
           bottom: int) -> Rect
    val x = int(real(left) / k)
    val y = int(real(top) / k)
    val w = int((real(pixel_w) - real(left) - real(right)) / k)
    val h = int((real(pixel_h) - real(top) - real(bottom)) / k)

    Rect(x, y, if w < 0 then 0 else w, if h < 0 then 0 else h)
end frame_rect
